prompt_messages.content:
[[ ## user_request ## ]]
Create a bar chart with city on the x-axis and weather.temp on the y-axis. Color the bars by weather.humidity.

[[ ## data_schema ## ]]
JSON Schema (10 rows):
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "id": {
      "type": "integer"
    },
    "city": {
      "type": "string"
    },
    "weather.temperature": {
      "type": "integer"
    },
    "weather.humidity": {
      "type": "integer"
    },
    "weather.wind_speed": {
      "type": "integer"
    },
    "readings.0": {
      "type": "integer"
    },
    "readings.1": {
      "type": "integer"
    },
    "readings.2": {
      "type": "integer"
    },
    "readings.3": {
      "type": "integer"
    },
    "readings.4": {
      "type": "integer"
    },
    "metadata.station": {
      "type": "string"
    },
    "metadata.elevation": {
      "type": "integer"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "city",
    "id",
    "metadata.elevation",
    "metadata.station",
    "readings.0",
    "readings.1",
    "readings.2",
    "readings.3",
    "readings.4",
    "weather.humidity",
    "weather.temperature",
    "weather.wind_speed"
  ]
}

Sample values:
  'id': [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
  'city': ['New York', 'Los Angeles', 'Chicago', 'Houston', 'Phoenix']
  'weather.temperature': [72, 85, 58, 92, 105]
  'weather.humidity': [55, 30, 65, 78, 12]
  'weather.wind_speed': [12, 8, 18, 6, 5]
  'readings.0': [68, 82, 55, 90, 102]
  'readings.1': [70, 84, 57, 91, 104]
  'readings.2': [72, 85, 58, 92, 105]
  'readings.3': [74, 86, 60, 93, 107]
  'readings.4': [71, 83, 56, 91, 103]
  'metadata.station': ['NYC-001', 'LAX-002', 'CHI-003', 'HOU-004', 'PHX-005']
  'metadata.elevation': [10, 71, 181, 15, 331]

IMPORTANT: This data has already been flattened. All fields are top-level with dot-separated names (e.g. 'employee.projects.startDate'). Do NOT use 'flatten' or 'fold' transforms. Reference field names directly.

Respond with the corresponding output fields, starting with the field `[[ ## reasoning ## ]]`, then `[[ ## vega_spec ## ]]`, and then ending with the marker for `[[ ## completed ## ]]`.

raw_response:
[[ ## reasoning ## ]] To create the bar chart as requested, we need to map the city to the x-axis, the weather temperature to the y-axis, and color the bars based on the weather humidity. This requires using the `bar` mark in Vega-Lite and specifying the appropriate encoding for x, y, and color.

[[ ## vega_spec ## ]] 
{
  "$schema": "https://vega.github.io/schema/vega-lite/v5.json",
  "data": {
    "values": [
      {"id": 1, "city": "New York", "weather.temperature": 72, "weather.humidity": 55},
      {"id": 2, "city": "Los Angeles", "weather.temperature": 85, "weather.humidity": 30},
      {"id": 3, "city": "Chicago", "weather.temperature": 58, "weather.humidity": 65},
      {"id": 4, "city": "Houston", "weather.temperature": 92, "weather.humidity": 78},
      {"id": 5, "city": "Phoenix", "weather.temperature": 105, "weather.humidity": 12}
    ]
  },
  "mark": "bar",
  "encoding": {
    "x": {
      "field": "city",
      "type": "nominal"
    },
    "y": {
      "field": "weather.temperature",
      "type": "quantitative"
    },
    "color": {
      "field": "weather.humidity",
      "type": "quantitative"
    }
  }
}

[[ ## completed ## ]]
